  "notes": "PRIMARY SOURCE: the club's own published scorecard images (Fairway Media / Bench Craft, (c)2022), linked from the SCORECARD tab at flamboroughhills.com/golf/. Every yardage, par and stroke index below was read directly off that card and each nine's yardages re-add exactly to the card's printed OUT totals for all six tees. SIX TEE SETS: Blue, Blue/White, White, White/Gold, Gold, Red. Blue/White and White/Gold are official combination tees (they mix forward/back tee boxes hole by hole), not separate tee decks - keep them if you want to match the printed card. Red is presented in the ladies' section of the card. RATINGS: the card publishes course rating/slope only per 18-hole COMBINATION, never per nine; those are in 'combinations'. The card does not label the ratings by gender; from the yardages the Red ratings read as women's ratings and the other five as men's, but that is inference, not printed on the card - treat gender labels as unverified. LADIES PAR: identical to men's on Hills (37) and Lakes (36), but on Woods holes 4 and 7 play as par 5 for ladies (men 4), giving ladies 38 vs men 36. Stroke index also differs by gender on all three nines. CROSS-CHECK: Golfify matches the official card hole-for-hole on ALL tees for Woods and Lakes, and on Hills matches Blue, Gold (which it labels 'Yellow'), every par and both stroke-index rows. mScorecard's 'Lakes to Hills' card independently reproduces the official Blue/White, White/Gold and Gold columns exactly for both Lakes and Hills. DISAGREEMENTS (official card used, aggregator noted): (1) Hills holes 1-3 White - card 295/466/151, Golfify 300/458/145 (card OUT 3190 vs Golfify 3181); (2) Hills holes 1-3 Red - card 260/382/145, Golfify 240/415/125 (card 2723 vs 2716); (3) mScorecard's Blue, White and Red columns are legacy data that do not match the 2022 card and were not used; (4) GolfPass lists Hills/Woods as 6356 yd, 70.3/123 - stale, superseded by the card's Blue 6486 yd, 71.1/131. BlueGolf's entry for this club is a placeholder ('Temporary', 18x100 yd) and carries no real data."
